Amenorrhoea (Primary/Secondary)
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Absence or cessation of menses, Signs of hypoestrogenism, Negative pregnancy test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-002
Reason for Visit: Acute distress secondary to sudden onset severe hot flashes, palpitations, and intense cyclic pelvic pain without menstrual bleeding, requiring urgent symptom control and assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute, severe cyclic pelvic pain associated with tachycardia and extreme psychological distress. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out acute anatomical obstruction or ectopic pregnancy and provide immediate symptom relief to prevent further deterioration before the next available in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have not had a period in four months, and tonight this lower stomach pain is unbearable, along with these intense hot flushes making my heart race.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms evolved over the past four months with cessation of established menses. Tonight, the patient developed acute, severe cyclic pelvic pain without bleeding. She reports significant recent weight loss and psychological stress at work. She has experienced worsening hot flashes and vaginal dryness over the last month, suggesting premature ovarian insufficiency. She denies galactorrhea or visual field defects. No hirsutism or acne noted.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently impaired by acute pelvic pain and distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: High stress levels related to work; no immediate safety hazards.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 54 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious, flushed, and mildly diaphoretic, pacing the room initially but now seated and guarding her pelvis.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft but exhibits deep tenderness in the suprapubic region; no rebound or rigidity. Speculum examination deferred due to acute distress, but external genitalia appears normal with subjective reports of atrophic vaginal mucosa. Urine pregnancy test is unequivocally negative.
Environmental Observations: Clean, organized living space with no acute hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications; no discrepancies noted.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Secondary Amenorrhoea (suspected Premature Ovarian Insufficiency with cyclic pain) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out due to negative point-of-care urine pregnancy test.

Polycystic Ovary Syndrome - Unlikely due to absence of hyperandrogenic signs such as hirsutism, acne, or obesity, and the presence of significant weight loss.

Prolactinoma - Unlikely given the absence of galactorrhea, normal peripheral vision on confrontation testing, and no chronic headaches.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, acute cyclic pelvic pain and marked autonomic symptoms (tachycardia, diaphoresis from hot flashes) in the context of secondary amenorrhoea. The objective clinical evidence of localized pelvic tenderness and distressing vasomotor symptoms, alongside a negative pregnancy test, necessitated urgent after-hours evaluation. Delayed assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in unmanaged acute pain and persistent severe psychological distress. This presentation strictly satisfies the criteria for an urgent after-hours visit to provide immediate analgesia and safely exclude life-threatening causes of acute pelvic pain.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral naproxen 500mg and paracetamol 1g for acute pelvic pain. Recommended comprehensive blood panel including follicle-stimulating hormone, estradiol, and prolactin levels to be drawn tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the frequency of hot flashes, keep a glass of ice water at the bedside and take small sips at the first sign of flushing; experiencing rapid cooling will reinforce the proactive management of vasomotor symptoms. To manage stress, practice deep breathing exercises for five minutes every morning; feeling calmer to start the day will build a reliable coping routine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 3 days for laboratory test coordination and long-term management planning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department immediately if the pelvic pain becomes sudden and sharp, if you develop a fever, or experience vomiting that prevents you from keeping fluids down.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending specific hormonal laboratory investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Acute pain should subside with the administered NSAIDs. Vasomotor symptoms will require ongoing hormonal evaluation and management.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you experience severe, worsening abdominal pain, fever, or an inability to tolerate oral fluids.
